module bindings.java.http_message_body_framing_conflict;

// RFC 7230 gives an HTTP/1 message exactly one body length, and says what to do
// when the headers offer two. Section 3.3.2: a message with more than one
// Content-Length field "MUST either reject the message as invalid or replace
// the duplicated field-values with a single valid Content-Length field".
// Section 3.3.3: a message carrying both Transfer-Encoding and Content-Length
// "might indicate an attempt to perform request smuggling ... and ought to be
// handled as an error", and a sender forwarding it "MUST remove the received
// Content-Length field". A parser that silently picks one of the two readings
// instead is not wrong on its own bytes -- it is wrong relative to the next
// implementation on the connection, which may pick the other, so one TCP stream
// becomes two different request sequences.
//
// The method this labels is the one where that obligation lands: the parser's
// header-block reader. It is identified by doing all three of the things the
// reconciliation needs, in one method -- it accumulates the parsed header lines
// into the message's header store, it asks whether the transfer encoding is
// chunked, and it asks for the content length -- because a method that only
// does one of them is not the place where the two framings meet. Netty
// documents both questions on io.netty.handler.codec.http.HttpUtil:
// isTransferEncodingChunked(HttpMessage) and getContentLength(HttpMessage,
// long); Servlet, Jetty and Undertow spell the same two as
// getHeader("Transfer-Encoding") / getContentLength(). Encoders, keep-alive
// policy helpers and the deprecated one-line delegates ask one of the two
// questions without ever building the header store, and are not reported.
//
// The negation lists the remedies RFC 7230 itself names, in the spellings a
// Java decoder uses for them: refusing the message (constructing a decoder or
// argument exception, marking the message invalid, failing the DecoderResult),
// and dropping the duplicate (removing the Content-Length header, or reading
// every value of it with getAll/getAllAsString so the count can be compared).
// It deliberately does not look for a specific comparison: a parser that
// rejects, or that normalises the header away, has discharged the obligation
// whichever way it spells the test. It is the absence of any of them, in a
// method that has both framings in hand, that is the finding.
//
// This is a property of the parser rather than of any value flowing through it
// -- there is no untrusted argument to follow, because the attacker's input is
// the header block the method is parsing -- so it is a presence fact on the
// method context, not a taint sink.
binding httpMessageBodyFramingConflict {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function" and containsAny(node.context.callPath, ["headers.add", "headers.set", "getHeaders.add", "message.headers.add", "trailer.headers.add"]) and containsAny(node.context.call, ["isTransferEncodingChunked", "isChunked"]) and containsAny(node.context.call, ["contentLength", "getContentLength", "isContentLengthSet"]) and not (containsAny(node.context.call, ["IllegalArgumentException", "DecoderException", "TooLongFrameException", "PrematureChannelClosureException", "invalidMessage", "getAll", "getAllAsString"])) and not (containsAny(node.context.callPath, ["headers.remove", "message.headers.remove", "DecoderResult.failure", "setDecoderResult"]))
  emit issue code.AmbiguousMessageBodyFraming at node
}
